{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Plasma cell neoplasms are diseases in which the body makes too many plasma cells.","source_span":"Plasma cell neoplasms are diseases in which the body makes too many plasma cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes (B cells), a type of white blood cell that is made in the bone marrow.","source_span":"Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes (B cells), a type of white blood cell that is made in the bone marrow."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Plasma cells in plasma cell neoplasms make an unneeded antibody protein called M protein.","source_span":"The plasma cells also make an antibody protein, called M protein, that is not needed by the body"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) is not cancer but can become cancer.","source_span":"Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) is not cancer but can become cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma, plasmacytoma, and multiple myeloma are types of plasma cell neoplasms that are cancer.","source_span":"The following types of plasma cell neoplasms are cancer: - Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma... - Plasmacytoma. - Multiple myeloma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"In MGUS, less than 10% of the bone marrow is made up of abnormal plasma cells and there is no cancer.","source_span":"less than 10% of the bone marrow is made up of abnormal plasma cells and there is no cancer"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"There are two types of plasmacytoma: isolated plasmacytoma of bone and extramedullary plasmacytoma.","source_span":"There are two types of plasmacytoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"In isolated plasmacytoma of bone, one plasma cell tumor is found in the bone, less than 10% of the bone marrow is plasma cells, and there are no other signs of cancer.","source_span":"In isolated plasmacytoma of bone, one plasma cell tumor is found in the bone, less than 10% of the bone marrow is made up of plasma cells, and there are no other signs of cancer."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Plasmacytoma of the bone often becomes multiple myeloma.","source_span":"Plasmacytoma of the bone often becomes multiple myeloma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Extramedullary plasmacytomas commonly form in tissues of the throat, tonsil, and paranasal sinuses.","source_span":"Extramedullary plasmacytomas commonly form in tissues of the throat, tonsil, and paranasal sinuses."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"In multiple myeloma, abnormal plasma cells (myeloma cells) build up in the bone marrow and form tumors in many bones of the body.","source_span":"abnormal plasma cells (myeloma cells) build up in the bone marrow and form tumors in many bones of the body"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Smoldering multiple myeloma is multiple myeloma that does not cause any signs or symptoms.","source_span":"Sometimes multiple myeloma does not cause any signs or symptoms. This is called smoldering multiple myeloma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"A multiple myeloma tumor can damage bone and cause hypercalcemia (too much calcium in the blood).","source_span":"A tumor can damage the bone and cause hypercalcemia (too much calcium in the blood)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Multiple myeloma and other plasma cell neoplasms may cause a condition called amyloidosis.","source_span":"Multiple myeloma and other plasma cell neoplasms may cause a condition called amyloidosis."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","statement":"Signs of amyloidosis include purple spots on the skin, an enlarged tongue, diarrhea, and tingling or numbness in the legs and feet.","source_span":"Purple spots on the skin. - Enlarged tongue. - Diarrhea. - Swelling caused by fluid in your body's tissues. - Tingling or numbness in your legs and feet."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[3,5],"question":"What is monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) and can it become cancer?","answer":"MGUS is a plasma cell neoplasm that is not cancer but can become cancer; in MGUS, less than 10% of the bone marrow is made up of abnormal plasma cells and there is no cancer at that stage."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[6,7,9],"question":"What are the two types of plasmacytoma and where does each typically occur?","answer":"The two types are isolated plasmacytoma of bone, where one tumor forms in the bone with less than 10% of bone marrow being plasma cells and no other cancer signs, and extramedullary plasmacytoma, where the tumor forms in soft tissue (commonly the throat, tonsil, or paranasal sinuses) rather than the bone or bone marrow."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What is smoldering multiple myeloma?","answer":"Smoldering multiple myeloma is multiple myeloma that does not cause any signs or symptoms; it may be found incidentally during a blood or urine test done for another condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What complication related to calcium levels can multiple myeloma cause?","answer":"Multiple myeloma tumors can damage bone and cause hypercalcemia, an excess of calcium in the blood, which can affect organs such as the kidneys, nerves, heart, muscles, and digestive tract."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[12,13],"question":"What is amyloidosis and what are two of its symptoms?","answer":"Amyloidosis is a condition, sometimes caused by multiple myeloma or other plasma cell neoplasms, in which antibody proteins build up in peripheral nerves and organs; symptoms can include purple spots on the skin and an enlarged tongue."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"Where do plasma cells come from, and what unneeded protein do they produce in plasma cell neoplasms?","answer":"Plasma cells develop from B lymphocytes, a type of white blood cell made in the bone marrow; in plasma cell neoplasms they produce an unneeded antibody protein called M protein."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000030_1","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What often happens to isolated plasmacytoma of the bone over time?","answer":"Plasmacytoma of the bone often progresses to become multiple myeloma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Neuroblastoma is a disease in which malignant cells form in neuroblasts (immature nerve tissue) in the adrenal gland, neck, chest, or spinal cord.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in neuroblasts (immature nerve tissue) in the adrenal gland, neck, chest, or spinal cord."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Neuroblastoma often begins in the nerve tissue of the adrenal glands.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma often begins in the nerve tissue of the adrenal glands."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"There are two adrenal glands, one on top of each kidney in the back of the upper abdomen.","source_span":"There are two adrenal glands, one on top of each kidney in the back of the upper abdomen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Neuroblastoma most often begins in infancy and may be diagnosed in the first month of life.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma most often begins in infancy and may be diagnosed in the first month of life."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"By the time neuroblastoma is diagnosed, the cancer has usually already metastasized (spread).","source_span":"By the time neuroblastoma is diagnosed, the cancer has usually metastasized (spread)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Neuroblastoma spreads most often to the lymph nodes, bones, bone marrow, and liver, and in infants also to the skin.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma spreads most often to the lymph nodes, bones, bone marrow, and liver. In infants, it also spreads to the skin."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Gene mutations that increase the risk of neuroblastoma are sometimes inherited from a parent to a child.","source_span":"Gene mutations that increase the risk of neuroblastoma are sometimes inherited (passed from the parent to the child)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"In children with an inherited gene mutation, neuroblastoma usually occurs at a younger age and more than one tumor may form in the adrenal glands.","source_span":"In children with a gene mutation, neuroblastoma usually occurs at a younger age and more than one tumor may form in the adrenal glands."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Common signs of neuroblastoma include a lump in the abdomen, neck, or chest, bulging eyes, dark circles around the eyes, and bone pain.","source_span":"Lump in the abdomen, neck, or chest. - Bulging eyes. - Dark circles around the eyes (\"black eyes\"). - Bone pain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"A higher than normal amount of VMA or HVA in urine catecholamine studies can be a sign of neuroblastoma.","source_span":"A higher than normal amount of VMA or HVA can be a sign of neuroblastoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"In an mIBG scan, radioactive mIBG is injected into a vein and scans may be taken over 1-3 days.","source_span":"Scans may be taken over 1-3 days."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"mIBG is used in high doses to treat neuroblastoma.","source_span":"mIBG is used in high doses to treat neuroblastoma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","statement":"Bone marrow aspiration and biopsy involves inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone to remove bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone.","source_span":"The removal of bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone by inserting a hollow needle into the hipbone or breastbone."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is neuroblastoma and where does it typically begin?","answer":"Neuroblastoma is a cancer in which malignant cells form in neuroblasts (immature nerve tissue); it most often begins in the adrenal glands but can also begin in the neck, chest, or spinal cord."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Can neuroblastoma be inherited, and how does it differ when it is?","answer":"Yes, neuroblastoma can be caused by a gene mutation inherited from a parent; in these children it usually occurs at a younger age and more than one tumor may form in the adrenal glands."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"Where does neuroblastoma most commonly spread by the time it is diagnosed?","answer":"By diagnosis, neuroblastoma has usually already metastasized, most often to the lymph nodes, bones, bone marrow, and liver; in infants it can also spread to the skin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What do urine catecholamine studies measure when diagnosing neuroblastoma?","answer":"They measure the amounts of VMA (vanillylmandelic acid) and HVA (homovanillic acid) in urine; a higher than normal amount of either can be a sign of neuroblastoma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[10,11],"question":"What is an mIBG scan used for in neuroblastoma, and how long can the scans take?","answer":"An mIBG scan uses radioactive mIBG injected into a vein to detect tumors such as neuroblastoma and monitor treatment response, with scans taken over 1 to 3 days; mIBG is also used in high doses as a treatment."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[12],"question":"How is a bone marrow aspiration and biopsy performed for neuroblastoma diagnosis?","answer":"A hollow needle is inserted into the hipbone or breastbone to remove bone marrow, blood, and a small piece of bone, which a pathologist then examines under a microscope for signs of cancer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"At what age does neuroblastoma most often begin?","answer":"Neuroblastoma most often begins in infancy and may be diagnosed within the first month of life."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"Neuroblastoma most often begins during early childhood, usually in children younger than 5 years of age.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma most often begins during early childhood, usually in children younger than 5 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"Neuroblastoma is the most common type of cancer in infants.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma is the most common type of cancer in infants."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"The number of new cases of neuroblastoma is greatest among children under 1 year of age.","source_span":"The number of new cases of neuroblastoma is greatest among children under 1 year of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"Neuroblastoma is slightly more common in males than in females.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma is slightly more common in males than females."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"The risk factors for neuroblastoma are not known.","source_span":"The risk factors for neuroblastoma are not known."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"In rare cases, neuroblastoma may be found before birth by fetal ultrasound.","source_span":"In rare cases, neuroblastoma may be found before birth, by fetal ultrasound."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"Neuroblastoma screening carries a risk of overdiagnosis.","source_span":"Neuroblastoma may be overdiagnosed."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"A false-negative neuroblastoma screening result may cause a person to delay seeking medical care even if symptoms are present.","source_span":"A person who receives a false-negative test result (one that shows there is no cancer when there really is) may delay seeking medical care even if there are symptoms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"A false-positive neuroblastoma screening result can cause anxiety and is usually followed by more tests and procedures.","source_span":"A false-positive test result (one that shows there is cancer when there really isn't) can cause anxiety and is usually followed by more tests and procedures, which also have risks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","statement":"A urine test showing a higher than normal amount of HVA or VMA can lead to tests and treatments for neuroblastoma that may not be needed.","source_span":"when a urine test result shows a higher than normal amount of homovanillic acid (HMA) or vanillyl mandelic acid (VMA), tests and treatments for neuroblastoma are likely to be done, but may not be needed"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"At what age does neuroblastoma most often begin, and how common is it among infant cancers?","answer":"Neuroblastoma most often begins during early childhood, usually before age 5, and it is the most common type of cancer in infants."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"In which age group are new neuroblastoma cases most frequent, and is it more common in one sex?","answer":"New cases of neuroblastoma are greatest among children under 1 year of age, and the disease is slightly more common in males than in females."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Are the risk factors for neuroblastoma known?","answer":"No, the risk factors for neuroblastoma are not known."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[6,9],"question":"What does \"overdiagnosis\" mean as a risk of neuroblastoma screening?","answer":"Overdiagnosis occurs when a screening test, such as a urine test showing higher than normal HVA or VMA, leads to diagnosis and treatment of neuroblastoma that may never have caused symptoms or become life-threatening, resulting in unneeded tests and treatments."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What risk does a false-negative neuroblastoma screening result carry?","answer":"A false-negative result shows no cancer when neuroblastoma is actually present, which can cause a person to delay seeking medical care even if symptoms exist."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What risk does a false-positive neuroblastoma screening result carry?","answer":"A false-positive result shows cancer when none is actually present, which can cause anxiety and is usually followed by more tests and procedures that carry their own risks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Can neuroblastoma be detected before a baby is born?","answer":"Yes, in rare cases neuroblastoma may be found before birth via fetal ultrasound."}
